.TH HU_TLS 3 "" "helios-unix" "Library functions"
.SH NAME
hu_tls_create, hu_tls_attach, hu_tls_attach_fd, hu_tls_handshake, hu_tls_handshake_wait, hu_tls_read, hu_tls_write, hu_tls_pending, hu_tls_info, hu_tls_interest, hu_tls_wait, hu_tls_shutdown, hu_tls_destroy \- use an owned TLS connection
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <helios/unix/tls.h>

struct hu_tls *hu_tls_create(const struct hu_tls_backend *backend,
    const void *configuration, const char *peer, unsigned timeout_ms);
int hu_tls_attach(struct hu_tls *tls,
                   const struct hu_tls_transport *transport);
int hu_tls_attach_fd(struct hu_tls *tls, int fd);
int hu_tls_handshake(struct hu_tls *tls);
int hu_tls_handshake_wait(struct hu_tls *tls);
int hu_tls_read(struct hu_tls *tls, void *buffer, unsigned size);
int hu_tls_write(struct hu_tls *tls, const void *buffer, unsigned size);
unsigned hu_tls_pending(struct hu_tls *tls);
int hu_tls_info(struct hu_tls *tls, struct hu_tls_info *info);
int hu_tls_interest(struct hu_tls *tls, int operation,
                     struct hu_tls_wait *interest);
int hu_tls_wait(struct hu_tls *tls, int operation, int timeout_ms);
int hu_tls_shutdown(struct hu_tls *tls);
void hu_tls_destroy(struct hu_tls *tls);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Connect a transport first, create a TLS object, attach the transport and
complete the handshake. Then send all application data through the object.
The API is independent of where encryption executes; do not expose a host
socket-upgrade interface to the application.
.PP
NULL backend selects the supplied host-offload provider; configuration is
provider-specific (use NULL for the default). peer is the expected server
hostname or IP address, copied by create. Its length must be 1..255 bytes.
timeout_ms is a handshake deadline in 100..120000 milliseconds. backend and
configuration must outlive the object. There is no plaintext fallback.
.SS Ownership
hu_tls_attach transfers transport ownership only on success. Its callback
table and context must remain valid while attached. See tls_backend.h for
provider/transport definitions.
.PP
hu_tls_attach_fd is the connected-descriptor convenience transport. It makes
the fd nonblocking and transfers ownership on success only. The fd must be
below FD_SETSIZE and must not already be registered with the TLS fd adapter.
After attachment use the object, not raw read/write/close on the fd.
This call does not register a hu_tls_fd adapter entry.
.SS Handshake and event loops
hu_tls_handshake returns 1 when ready, 0 when pending, or -1 on failure.
hu_tls_handshake_wait drives negotiation synchronously and returns 0 on
success or -1 on failure. Its deadline includes time between handshake calls.
.PP
Read and write return transferred byte counts, or -1 with errno set. Read
returns 0 at end of stream. Handle short writes. On EAGAIN, obtain interest
for the operation that blocked, wait, then retry. A read can require transport
writability and a write can require readability. Preserve unaccepted output
while retrying; advance the buffer only by bytes actually accepted.
.PP
hu_tls_interest fills struct hu_tls_wait with fd, events (HU_TLS_WAIT_READ
and/or HU_TLS_WAIT_WRITE) and timeout_ms (-1 means no provider timer).
Operations are HU_TLS_READ, HU_TLS_WRITE, HU_TLS_HANDSHAKE and HU_TLS_SHUTDOWN.
Include both the requested fd readiness and provider timer in the event loop.
hu_tls_pending reports already decrypted bytes; consume these without waiting
for more wire input.
.PP
hu_tls_wait is a convenience wait for an operation. Its timeout is milliseconds;
-1 imposes no caller deadline. It returns 1 for readiness or a due provider
timer, 0 for the caller's timeout, or -1 with errno set. A wake means retry the
operation, not that negotiation or I/O has completed.
.SS Status and closing
hu_tls_info copies state, provider error, protocol, cipher and diagnostic
message. States are HU_TLS_NEGOTIATING, HU_TLS_READY, HU_TLS_FAILED and
HU_TLS_CLOSED. Provider error is diagnostic information, distinct from errno.
.PP
hu_tls_shutdown sends accepted output and TLS close notification, returning
1 complete, 0 pending or -1 failure. Drive pending shutdown with its readiness
interest and an application deadline. hu_tls_destroy cancels immediately and
releases the owned transport exactly once; it does not promise graceful
shutdown. NULL is accepted. Use each object from one event loop, not concurrently.
.SH EXAMPLE
Take a connected fd and return a ready TLS object. This example consumes the
fd on every path; the attachment API itself consumes it only on success.
.EX
#include <helios/unix/tls.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>

struct hu_tls *secure_connection(int fd, const char *peer)
{
    struct hu_tls *tls;
    int error;
    tls = hu_tls_create(0, 0, peer, 10000);
    if (!tls) {
        error = errno; close(fd); errno = error; return 0;
    }
    if (hu_tls_attach_fd(tls, fd) < 0) {
        error = errno;
        close(fd); hu_tls_destroy(tls); errno = error; return 0;
    }
    if (hu_tls_handshake_wait(tls) < 0) {
        error = errno;
        hu_tls_destroy(tls); errno = error; return 0;
    }
    return tls;
}
.EE
.SH PROVIDERS
The default requires the Linux I/O server's TLS service, with certificate
trust configured on that host. It verifies the peer and supports hostname
indication. It does not expose client certificates, ALPN or an insecure mode.
No native Transputer cryptographic provider is included.
.SH RETURN VALUE
create returns a pointer or NULL with errno set. Attach, info and interest
return 0 or -1 with errno set. Handshake, wait, I/O and shutdown conventions
are described above. Failed negotiation remains failed; do not retry as plaintext.
.SH SEE ALSO
hu_tls_fd(3), getaddrinfo(3), helios-unix(7)
